A||||16||Dan87GT||Metal can, passeger side rear wheelwell?||||1
Z||000000||Dan87GT||10-17-1999||03:25 PM||||What is the metal container that is located under the passenger-side rear wheelwell? Is it some sort of overflow container, or for fumes?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-17-1999||04:37 PM||||It is the vapor recovery canister. It keeps fuel tank vapor from reaching the air. The 2 vaccum lines coming off it purge out the vapor when the car is running.<P>BTW..... There is a filter on the bottom of the canister. make sure the tray under the canister is not full of crap, and that the filter is able to breath. You have to remove the clamp bolt to release the canister..... takes about 5 minutes. The part number for the filter, should it be trashed, is Fram #BA3865. The filter is about a dollar.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||10-17-1999||05:37 PM||||I thought the fuel vapor canister was that "can" just next to the air filter on the driver side.  I have an '88 Formula, did they move them that year, is it in a different spot in the GT, or are we just talking about two different things?||||reg||5
Z||000003||theogre||10-17-1999||08:24 PM||||337 years old and still can't tell my right from my up.......<P>wrong can.... yes. the one with 3 lines on the drivers side is what I said above.<P>If the lines from the one you are looking at are about 3/8 inch steel and screw in then it sounds like the fuel filter,,,, which is huge in these cars. <P>If it's ribbed and looks like a soup/jiuce can, then it's likely a vacuum reserve canister.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||10-17-1999||11:37 PM||||If it is the fuel filter, it's a good idea to change those about every 10,000 miles.  Let me tell you about the time I replaced a fuel filter with over 180,000 miles on it...||||reg||6
Z||000005||Richard Parnell||10-18-1999||03:35 PM||||Fiero56, I would like to know more about this mysterious "can" on the driver side of engine compartment.  I see fuel lines running back and forth between it and the injectors.  My manual says nothing about this unit, only that I should clean my fuel filter every so often.  I can't find a fuel filter so I figure this big "can" has soething to do with it.  Someone once told me not to touch the thing but I'm sure it must need some maintenance.  Suggestions?||||reg||6
Z||000006||fiero56||10-18-1999||06:48 PM||||Your fuel filter is just next to the heat shield for the catalytic convertor.  When I change mine, I do it during an oil change.  I put my car up on ramps, crawl under from behind, and it is just next to the cat.  Not a hard job to do once you get to it, maybe a little easier than replacing the oil filter which is even farther up, above the cat. I'm sure theogre knows more about these evaportative canistors than I do, but here's what I know.  It is designed to store vapors that come from the gas tank and fuel system.  This "can" is filled with charcoal and is connected to:fuel tank, throttle body, and intake.  After the engine is started these vapors are then used up, (kind of a "keep the air clean" emissions thing)  <P><BR>I just grabbed my Haynes manual and it tells everything about it on page:165, under EECS (Evaporative Emission Control System).  I'm sure Ogre can ellaborate more on this if needed.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-18-1999).]||||reg||10
Z||000007||theogre||10-18-1999||10:15 PM||||Not much more to say about the evap canister.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> It's entire purpose is to collect fuel tank vapor. It might have an extra line on the V6 to collect intake vapor. The evap canister doesn't need much to keep it working. <P>There is a "tray" under it that protects it's filter. You want to make sure the tray is free of leaves and crap once in a while. The filter is also replaceable if it's crapped up real bad. you just pull out the old one, and tuck the edges of the new one under the rim on the canister. (you have to remove the bolt from the mounting clamp to move the canister to check the tray.)<P>As F56 said, It's filled with charcoal. The way it works is the line from the fuel tank feeds in vapor that would either escape into the air or preasurize the hell out of the fuel tank. The bottom of the thing is open to the air which lets out pressure while the charcoal holds the fuel vapor.<P>The 2 vac lines go to diferant places on the engine intake. One goes to a "ventury vacuum" port. This line operates a valve that only opens when the gas pedal is beyond a certain point. The other line, which goes to manifold vacuum, then can suck out the vapor buildup in the canister, and draw fresh air in thru the bottom filter.<P>The canister can be a source of both rich mix errors in the ECM, and vacuum leaks. When checking for either problem, always block the lines from the canister. If the canister is causing the problem, it has to be replaced to comply with federal law. It can be blocked off without affecting performance, but please don't do that. Find another canister in the scrap yard.<P>BTW, This canister is why you shouldn't stuff, top off, the fuel tank. If you force liquid into it you'll likely wreck it, and cause rich mix errors. This didn't used to be such an issue, but some of these new "vapor recovery" fuel pumps can pressurize the tank pretty good.<P>Change the fuel filter every 10K miles, probly not a bad idea. Wish I knew where the hell mine was. It moved on the 87 4 cyls.... I think it's up under the battery some place... can't see it under the car. (Fuel plumbing for 87 4 cyl is completly diferant from the older 4 cyl cars.) Haven't had time to get all the way under......<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-18-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||superXmotorsports||10-18-1999||11:20 PM||||Ogre, is there any way to bypass the canister all together without causing bad signals to the computer or service codes?  I would like to eliminate it on my race car I'm building that dosen't have to be inspected or go to emissions.  Thanks.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000009||theogre||10-19-1999||09:34 AM||||I doubt you'll get any performance change at all. A properly working canister should make no diferance in performance wether it's there or not.<P>Simply blocking the vauum lines would disable it. You still need it or something to serve as an end for the fuel tank vent. You can't plug the tank vent, and you can't just leave it hang unless you like driving a fireball.<P>As for wheight the thing is only about 1 pound.<P>Unless you can find some measurable improvment in performance, and I don't really think you will, I'd say leave it working. It's a safety device as much as an emmisions device. It prevents fuel pressure from building up in the tank and vapor build up under the car. Those vapors have to go some place, better to know where.<P>It's designed to be vented slowly when ever the car runs, and not screw up the mixture in the intake. It doesn't purge all at once. the purge valve only opens above a certain percent of throttle opening.<P>The purge valve can be tested. to test it you need two vacuum pumps, one on each line. First pump down the large line a few inches, you don't need much, you just want to see it take a vacuum and not leak. 6-10 inches vacuum is enough.<P>then pump the down the small line. at some fairly small vacuum number the valve will open, and the vacuum in the large line will disappear.<P>if it doesn't check the lines.... the small one goes to the top of the valve, the large to the bottom. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Shaun41178||10-20-1999||03:20 AM||||GUYS: He said passenger side of the car not drivers side.  The fuel canistor is on the drivers side and so is the coffee can looking thing which holds vacuum.  Again he said passenger side.  now perhaps he meant drivers side but Dan87Gt ahs not posted anotehr comment on this thread so I still have to assume that he means passenger side.<P>So Dan to answer your question, i have no idea what this thing is on your car if it is where you say it is which is on the passenger side.  The only major thing on the passenger side of the engien is the battery that is not directly connected to the engine.  As far as a container under the passenger side rear wheel well, I have no idea what that could be.  Any lines coming out of it and if so where do these lines or wires of some sort lead to or seem to lead to?  Perhaps someone before you that owned the car put something on the car that did not come with it from the factory.  Need some more details or for you to say that you meant the drivers side of the car.  <P>Of course if you are in Australia and have had your car converted to right hand drive then our drivers side here in the U.S would be your passenger side.  That would be cool actually.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||10-20-1999||08:32 AM||||I figured it was solved when Ogre mentioned the fuel filter since 87GT never responded.  It must be the fuel filter, because as you said, there is nothing else down there unless it was added to the car.(I assumed this was on a car a v-6,  because of his s/n.||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||10-20-1999||09:42 AM||||We know he was originaly talking about the passenger side........ I said that above already,,, but like most threads we digressed a little. We even already covered that depending on what's connected to it, it's either the vac can, or the fuel filter.... Then since I mistakenly mentioned the vapor can, some one wanted to know more about it too......<P>There's not much to say about filters or vac cans,,,,, if they are screwed up or dirty you get another one.....<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-20-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Dan87GT||10-20-1999||12:45 PM||||It is on the passenger side of the car under the front of the rear wheelwell. You can see some of the can from the engine compartment,there is a whole in the frame right next to the battery, you can see the silver can. Both lines run under the battery tray, one goes to the fuel canister on the driver side. <BR>   My biggest concern about this can was that the lines were shot and needed to be replaced, I was surprised to see it there, And wasn't sure of its function.||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||10-20-1999||03:54 PM||||that one is definatly the vac reserve can. It "stores" vacuum so that when manifold vacuum is low things like the cruise control will continue to work properly.<P>I believe the Fiero one has a check valve on top in the port that is connected to manifold vacuum. (the line that goes to the vapor can in your case.) you should only be able to draw air from the vac can, not blow into it. I'm not sure,,,, It's posible the check valve is in the line to the vapor can, or may be on the vapor can, but there is definitly a chack valve.<P>make sure you test the can that it is holding vacuum. They have a nasty habbit of having a rust pin hole in them.<P>If the can rusts out, I usually dig around in the salvage yard for one of the plastic ball ones instead of putting in another metal one. Just make sure that if the can has a check valve, that the ball one does too. Other wise you'll need to put a check in the vac line. If in doubt use a ball with the check valve.<P>For most things, the size of the replacement doesn't matter much. A little smaller has never mattered on anything I've done. Bigger certainly won't hurt at all.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Eddie||10-20-1999||07:38 PM||||Hay Theogre<BR>	The gas filter is 2 feet in front of the cat. right in the middle of the car you can reach it from the left side.	EDDIE||||reg||12
Z||000017||theogre||10-20-1999||08:18 PM||||Thanks Eddie, I'll look there... haven't been that far up under it yet.||||reg||1
